findings.description:
Three per-agent Python loops remain after this slice:
- the query loop in `tick.py:48-52`;
- `build_neighbor_data`, which is described as "a single pass to pad, mask, and self-exclude" (line 101). The algorithm is unspecified. The naive version loops over agents doing `neighbors != idx` and a row copy. A loop-free construction exists: concatenate the lists, compare against `np.repeat(eval_indices, lengths)`, then scatter by within-row offset. The design should pick one and say so;
- the `max_speeds` comprehension (see the previous finding).

The slice plan promises "O(1) Python overhead regardless of agent count" (`100-slices.behavior-engine.md:37`). The design softens this to "constant Python overhead" (line 19) and "eliminates the Python loop overhead entirely" (line 23). Neither holds.

Line 45 and line 184 assign query-loop vectorization to slice 105 and say 105 "introduces a batch query". The 105 plan entry (`100-slices.behavior-engine.md:34`) says only "a spatial hash grid behind the existing query interface". It plans no batch interface. As written, nothing owns the remaining loop. Either state the residual cost honestly and note that 105 must be extended, or put a vectorized builder in scope.

The claim that the 10-15 ms overrun comes from force loops is not backed by a per-stage measurement. The server tick duration (`src/migratory_server/tick.py:81-86`) covers more than `engine.tick()`. The remaining steps still scale with agent count:
- the per-agent brute-force queries, O(n²) overall;
- the new builder;
- the environment hooks and serialization.

The walkthrough is a 5-minute manual run with no pass threshold beyond "stay within budget". It doesn't say where to read the result. The server logs a "Tick N overran budget" warning and increments `metrics.overrun_count`. A pre-slice per-stage timing (query, build, forces) and a micro-benchmark of `tick()` on a clustered 100-agent state would make the success criterion checkable and would show whether the forces were the cause.
